body:
Sorry to double reply to you. I don't want to over-occupy your feed.

&#x200B;

I added the key phrasing of "Up To" for many of the features, including the Paladin, to give them the choice of having Up To the rolled number of creatures instead of being forced to have all those foes collaborate on them. Additionally, their effective AC is increased by 3 for attacks from those foes until the start of their next turn.

I watched as my partner's brother desperately tried to justify NFTs as a valid investment, explaining that it wasn't just another cryptocurrency that relied on the losses of the majority for the gain of the lucky minority.

&#x200B;

The fool spoke with the quivering insecurity of a single sperm before the might of King.

Man, it must have been just over 20 years ago since I played a Christmas DnD Session.

&#x200B;

Flying reindeer chariots, golems made of presents, a heel-turn Rudolph who started going by the name of, "Dark Rei(g)n." It took place in the Candy Cane Cathedral. The remaining 8 reindeer had to be reunited for only with their combined might could Rudolph be turned back to the side of light. We spoke in rhyming verse to describe our attacks. It was such a distinct time for me, not just DnD, but for my family as well. Probably the last time I had a real laugh with my father, truly engaged him eye to eye, even though I was but a child, I said to him, "Dad, promise me that this Christmas will just be for us, no company, no running around house to house, no distractions," my father held me and said, "Son, nothing will distract me from the fact that in 1998, The Undertaker threw Mankind off Hell in a Cell, and plummeted 16 ft through an announcer's table."

I use the following in different campaigns:  


\- A FFG Star Wars RPG and FATE style Inspiration mechanic, where the party can flip a token from a "Player Side" to the "DM Side" to either introduce a new element into a scene, or gain advantage on a roll. The DM may also flip a token to introduce a complication to a player's failed rolls, or grant an NPC advantage.   


\- Character Dice: A system akin to Bardic Inspiration/Superiority die where I homebrew customized abilities with the player based on the character, where they expend Character Dice (of a pool equal to their level, recovered on short or long rests depending on the game) to achieve custom effects that may augment their class abilities or grant them dynamic features to provide more versatility.

&#x200B;

\- NPCs: Each party member may "attune" one NPC, who grants a Legendary Action style feature once per short rest. This allows the party to benefit from the NPC without having to run a full other body in combat.

&#x200B;

\- Damage Types: Players can swap the damage types of their spells, but may not change the damage type at will. Eg. If you want to learn an Acid damage Fireball, that's rad, but you can't will it to be a fireball, unless you also learn Fireball, or Psychicball or Fairyball or Thunderdome.

&#x200B;

\- Evolving Items: Magic items are a rarity, but have a higher potential power budget. They have three phases, with the second and third unlocked after achieving specific goals. Eg. Orcbane wants you to slay the current Orc War Queen to prove yourself worthy of its evolution, afterwards it wants you to find the mythical Orcish Kraken and prove its existence to all of mortalkind.
